[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420931a7-bfe9-57fd-be28-96ee8c3e7a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550522} {:answer/id #uuid "52b2408a-7f36-5c00-b12f-5d5d12445119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550564} {:answer/id #uuid "7676748a-b3e1-574b-8bbc-7af2c704efdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550665} {:answer/id #uuid "847735f3-85e7-556f-ab13-996c4f671091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550702} {:answer/id #uuid "8a774edc-0a83-5eff-b7f3-8dd8b4849f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a774edc-0a83-5eff-b7f3-8dd8b4849f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550718} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6a43897-150e-534f-a498-9f892cf924c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f16c2b9-1c8b-50a4-93ab-bbcc2578659f" :question/math-academy-id "q-5994" :question/problem "$3{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${30}^{∘}$ and ${45}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have the  values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}$ simply means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘} &= 3{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})}^{2}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ &= 3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ &= 3(\\frac{3}{9})-1 \\\\ &= 1-1 \\\\ &= 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a6ccf5-14ce-5806-ace1-7ca838a6fd7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a6ccf5-14ce-5806-ace1-7ca838a6fd7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550943} :answer-field/id #uuid "82fa694a-25f2-5e7d-ab30-eca894f443f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24a538ad-232b-5718-acdd-ce07c94cc6d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-274506" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})+(-\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{55}}{18}-\\frac{10}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b0de47-6d93-56f4-9eec-48894f36d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550384} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4fe516-3675-584c-b3d4-a4182956369b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550503} {:answer/id #uuid "72ba0ba6-9d3d-570e-859e-0393addd7b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550656} {:answer/id #uuid "7400282a-889c-5443-9fca-18dc0075e6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550659} {:answer/id #uuid "f948c330-b81c-5ef2-94de-f3c68097c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{13}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b0de47-6d93-56f4-9eec-48894f36d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550384} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c3804a-ba42-5eda-8d70-9caba3ae75d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29930a9c-51d8-56d8-87b6-15535f5105d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124392" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e597e317f32a2a9a3757ce583a2b053f4cba1da1a2119c572671e7edc96c61e4.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{6}{7},\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})}{(\\frac{6}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ff4504-ac7a-57d3-a336-b4500ec61972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff4504-ac7a-57d3-a336-b4500ec61972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550820} :answer-field/id #uuid "598d1261-b549-515b-a77a-1a37d5829749" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e61a3ba-7f7b-576c-9651-31afc3ef6f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-326306" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(-\\frac{3}{5})+(\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{65}+\\frac{48}{65} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55053340-f635-546e-8e5d-4621e256edb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550570} {:answer/id #uuid "ab11db4e-65b6-5238-aa40-285ffcaa4e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550828} {:answer/id #uuid "b7737c7e-9824-5e5f-a3ae-34856a098e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550872} {:answer/id #uuid "efdbe697-d6a3-508c-a692-ae48f85ba690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efdbe697-d6a3-508c-a692-ae48f85ba690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551016} :answer-field/id #uuid "319f9cc5-6350-50bc-9ef5-9fc966e9afe8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3542e3-4bf3-5d25-87cb-feba5f3ffd90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550425} {:answer/id #uuid "5558f4a3-3994-5897-8ff9-622d5df04392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550571} {:answer/id #uuid "e29b2edf-998e-5740-bb95-54727e9f45f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e29b2edf-998e-5740-bb95-54727e9f45f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550986} :answer-field/id #uuid "44914850-8fa0-5396-b873-81fa2f52fbd0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ca47af-c6a7-509f-8d83-2150c0238a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550533} {:answer/id #uuid "92481f36-1e85-521a-ba9a-acee004b176f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550736} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c34785-8282-5217-a833-be2afab68c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92481f36-1e85-521a-ba9a-acee004b176f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550736} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd62ed7-f5a0-5d14-b295-af477d1b5a66" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "336eb7d8-27df-584e-adc9-df5d1de8bc4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344058" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{9}\\cdot (-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d65c081f-46e7-50d2-b0af-c99aac626906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d65c081f-46e7-50d2-b0af-c99aac626906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550948} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1767cf0-549a-5130-876d-9403ed25774d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44100483-c880-5325-8cb2-5b4178bd1ae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-267389" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe2f1266db9f675a2c82803728d37f53d0995c25cf61f74304a731c3d21c64c8.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\frac{5}{6})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/489a919487ffe7051a27f736ff8ca210e4c246bceac42055e31f5150bc4e094b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431f436d-286f-5124-9f57-838cbb663593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550525} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d170e4-09b8-54a6-95dc-732e3918ff88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550908} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d301bb-3dab-58d3-b3c7-a4b5e4f95d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550936} {:answer/id #uuid "f71b7119-0fb5-5212-83f2-49afd258fc8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551032} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6b2054-d008-528a-bf1c-110725aeb7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71b7119-0fb5-5212-83f2-49afd258fc8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551032} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2abad3-93fb-5e4a-a512-cc96b4e71bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4643f6f8-6def-57fc-9f05-9106c1781f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-141389" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}-b\\sqrt{7}}{8},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}-1\\sqrt{7}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b60d51-d24a-5296-b05c-ca2de01453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4b60d51-d24a-5296-b05c-ca2de01453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550944} :answer-field/id #uuid "9194ee95-2b5a-5c6e-af69-3efb185447ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5237ac2b-249d-5de5-a0f0-312a03200ea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-267419" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8fc1b09d51bf0d7ac526a917b2341015b787b3098627bedd25e1638ecf041cc.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fd/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00426310-f76d-56f6-ba30-c47b331278a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550368} {:answer/id #uuid "40f2638c-2ac7-53e0-a8a3-97b62783762e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le -9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550518} {:answer/id #uuid "4b95ea89-bff8-543e-8bf5-469472004602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550546} {:answer/id #uuid "9f72d829-4271-58e8-a988-e7422040bc4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550777} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2f9355-cedb-5f9d-876c-a0aae0d73911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00426310-f76d-56f6-ba30-c47b331278a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550368} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4a26f0-ed0b-57e8-a627-d6b00292461a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66604494-d5d9-5eb8-81e3-a76e00656f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-154008" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{y}{3}\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,3 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-3)\\cdot 3 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -9 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}3)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -9 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & \\ge -9 \\\\ y & \\ge -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge -9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e05cb0-0d11-5a48-ae94-2df779dd5f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})(\\frac{s}{5}-\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550494} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3da440-df14-5563-8357-d5962df16751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{s}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550508} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0dd266-8b12-51fc-9c32-a952e58010e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550631} {:answer/id #uuid "df3163ca-87f7-559b-8a74-14ac6aab05d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{9}+\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{s}{9}-\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550974} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f16648-6db8-53b7-95d5-df7ac3de4c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{s}{5}-\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e05cb0-0d11-5a48-ae94-2df779dd5f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})(\\frac{s}{5}-\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550494} :answer-field/id #uuid "77716a4d-fef6-5073-82d5-f3b9f059ff15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "840068df-b5bb-5a8c-a125-569067cf40da" :question/math-academy-id "q-111663" :question/problem "$\\frac{{s}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{1}{81}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{1}{81}={(\\frac{s}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{s}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})(\\frac{s}{5}-\\frac{1}{9}) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78061c99-9116-56c2-88f4-777413d48782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550672} {:answer/id #uuid "85bccb7d-0294-553d-b094-d5837f950d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550705} {:answer/id #uuid "f77c27c9-c7d3-591e-bac3-638faae40001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85bccb7d-0294-553d-b094-d5837f950d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550705} :answer-field/id #uuid "34398e9c-a70c-53b7-b84a-4a5e338eee91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30256479-a0c3-536f-992c-58c727330693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550484} {:answer/id #uuid "644342fd-f897-5f58-92a1-0b8ef53eb76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550609} {:answer/id #uuid "ab325d87-a065-546e-baf7-139015307fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab325d87-a065-546e-baf7-139015307fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550829} :answer-field/id #uuid "819e15d7-2e20-52a4-83ba-4e170679fa89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a503430-335a-52ca-a8f2-1a25c8b18de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343819" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{2}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{3{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d91780-96a0-5b05-9282-837d0bc5cf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550411} {:answer/id #uuid "23fabac7-34be-5901-889d-cbd6475a113f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550454} {:answer/id #uuid "87f86819-67b7-5681-bbb3-b9db74260b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550711} {:answer/id #uuid "9975a3bb-76fc-5c77-ae49-5724d89ccc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550757} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b4e39f-b8d2-5d43-9273-97be2dbccbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23fabac7-34be-5901-889d-cbd6475a113f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550454} :answer-field/id #uuid "826a30ca-938a-5809-9560-1e4fb2a1a3e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97834400-1835-5bc3-9180-5bacac383a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119317" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{1}4}{6},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1\\sqrt{14}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=1,$ and $a+b=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a040c0f-a1fd-58ed-aad2-fe5b5cf047d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a040c0f-a1fd-58ed-aad2-fe5b5cf047d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7103d3e-80a2-5de4-ac0e-a10a2142cb05" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1710ed1-4caf-58bf-82be-5d414220e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1710ed1-4caf-58bf-82be-5d414220e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550984} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed949f4a-0931-51ac-8da5-094e551428fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ecd9133-5002-52de-bbff-55ef2618e8b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-326383" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{7}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $52$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16}-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{4})-(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}+\\frac{12\\sqrt{7}}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12\\sqrt{7}}{52}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2091824b-6f43-593c-84fe-25de29e7e4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550441} {:answer/id #uuid "4da788ef-fc41-5934-85c7-6c54e9d66986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ac3d1-fd3a-5663-99f5-0b6e86c9d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550690} {:answer/id #uuid "8a76f1ca-01f8-5917-8e75-64c4de462b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{165}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550717} {:answer/id #uuid "8e788705-609a-5a7a-af02-0595198b67ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{113}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ac3d1-fd3a-5663-99f5-0b6e86c9d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550690} :answer-field/id #uuid "be770eee-142d-5138-82b0-d774ea34700b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3aefa5f-7730-50af-bdda-78228d7a1e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-143572" :question/problem "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. Box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. And box $C$ contains $10$ light bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly selected from the box. Find the probability that the bulb is defective." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A,$ $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$ and $2$  defective bulbs out of $10$ bulbs in box $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P(D|C)=\\frac{2}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{2}{10})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{2}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{90}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d409728-f16b-5dbd-989e-4297f678c27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550481} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87b11b-03eb-50d1-a061-8f5cba3a6ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550548} {:answer/id #uuid "505afb7e-87e3-5258-8fa9-547c7b39ab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550557} {:answer/id #uuid "958cbb92-f043-53d1-bb9b-025a90326fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550744} {:answer/id #uuid "bde96f49-8d44-5f49-af06-0fedc038fdc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958cbb92-f043-53d1-bb9b-025a90326fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550744} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88de4d6-11e4-59ab-b35f-5b9cbf6e3f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7374391-644b-5fe8-92d1-774c6f2685b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141359" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}}{4},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=2,$ and $a+b=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df83a4e1-66ba-5873-97da-2d74aacdb5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df83a4e1-66ba-5873-97da-2d74aacdb5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550977} :answer-field/id #uuid "24922baa-2702-5f4a-9cd9-809ee303d03c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fa37d87-2d10-5b67-a6d0-78d0e0a5a54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa37d87-2d10-5b67-a6d0-78d0e0a5a54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550513} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab314b19-c35e-5b2b-9cf9-5dbd3ed37713" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb1f9724-9227-5181-ae8d-25a299447d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326377" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\sqrt{3}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $34$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{15}{17})(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{34}-\\frac{15}{34} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}-15}{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0f4507-f336-56f6-86cc-8b6c67aa2f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550435} {:answer/id #uuid "77a7b2c9-147e-5fc7-9ea6-9c47b3f3c127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550669} {:answer/id #uuid "cf06079f-488c-54c6-9f67-6f54375dcb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550926} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5c049f-d4ae-506d-8359-38c91b104d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550968} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e4f716-0f36-5041-8ce8-3e1332c0fd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf06079f-488c-54c6-9f67-6f54375dcb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550926} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b2f076d-ce9b-57f9-98c4-9fb84d87fd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbced5cb-cb8f-554f-bc5c-eb4739e6f20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119320" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{2}{3})(-\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{21}-\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01413d91-53a9-5c2f-95f0-dd975a28a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550372} {:answer/id #uuid "4178c511-d518-5d4a-9339-82483116d69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550521} {:answer/id #uuid "6e66f12f-7817-5b79-8dae-5467ced5edfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550642} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f5e68-f562-519c-8030-f810985e9c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55f5e68-f562-519c-8030-f810985e9c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550801} :answer-field/id #uuid "07ef0176-9972-561f-b028-e97ddd2d78db" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8777b0ac-3a0e-5d31-a627-3b0c9dbebe84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550709} {:answer/id #uuid "99bfd701-6515-5005-8a6e-47fa0952ac7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eve