\\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =7 \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{9}{2}))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{7})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{9}{2},$ $b=1,$ and $r=\\sqrt{7}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{9}{2},1)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05deb54f-1278-5d90-a4df-cb11ac5345fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554916} {:answer/id #uuid "0f911ec9-43c1-57c5-8bc3-386823389a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,\\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554919} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd20098-d6fa-5aac-a27d-dfb6dd53ec8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554925} {:answer/id #uuid "4f47e046-6d0e-59a0-9491-42bc567168bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554931} {:answer/id #uuid "e326265a-ef5b-5fa5-a1d8-70164b421f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-3,\\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05deb54f-1278-5d90-a4df-cb11ac5345fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554916} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6a6ce4-7edc-5db9-9755-1bc6927c7c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73554832-d205-5c91-af0d-110fdbd01485" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-3)^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=6?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{3}{2}))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=3,$ $b=- \\frac{3}{2},$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41eb3881-27fe-5aa3-9e5e-d8094356821e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554928} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb35869-a632-5bb5-bc7a-4c4f84e3cca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554959} {:answer/id #uuid "ca282382-0037-5a62-9b81-40739d2aa75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{5}{3},\\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554972} {:answer/id #uuid "f1954577-1420-5f95-b0fd-4b51291d3078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554979} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9211b3-e46e-5cf6-b678-fafa2ce94c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $72$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1954577-1420-5f95-b0fd-4b51291d3078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554979} :answer-field/id #uuid "c699ae23-4ea3-52d8-bcad-f2bd30023082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "915cc9d8-786e-5928-932b-d3b73bca3d09" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}=72?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{7}{2}))^{2} & =(6 \\sqrt{2})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{72}=\\sqrt{36\\cdot 2}=6 \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=- \\frac{7}{2},$ and $r=6 \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and its radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555029} {:answer/id #uuid "10021868-bebf-5d55-88d6-1666034c1a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555031} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef13b49-b8a1-573d-960c-ebf7e8cd471e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555056} {:answer/id #uuid "df0e68c0-8aa4-5c57-a44f-b03b70691336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555543} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bcd3f9-a718-5576-984c-731f9180bd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555029} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea4d1f3-3230-5c90-8f5f-f708753efce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-143774" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d6bf9d-c36b-57f4-a4a3-d6d10d20a9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555078} {:answer/id #uuid "2d113435-7d4d-5e64-bf7a-6096265af4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555100} {:answer/id #uuid "97a99f95-7b5c-511f-b1dd-63628675fb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555361} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555414} {:answer/id #uuid "b15e8623-fcd9-53a6-8695-28d9aee82949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555414} :answer-field/id #uuid "89806148-2b04-5dff-9c9d-9aecabad8125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cf3361e-986e-5750-bf9f-73762dff3b0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106771" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e4a15a-eda1-52a1-8baa-9cdce6bed32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555063} {:answer/id #uuid "2718af2e-0e58-5f94-b179-4b6122d8bbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555083} {:answer/id #uuid "84737793-7f74-5db9-b6ff-72f64548ee3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555306} {:answer/id #uuid "959f0371-d54d-54ee-88dc-a7c52e86bcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555355} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f0f01f-aa87-552e-8444-03f833352b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2718af2e-0e58-5f94-b179-4b6122d8bbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555083} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abcfe54-e8c7-5c1d-9465-cff1e4813088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "206046fc-72eb-5e7e-8330-c7dfc01fc134" :question/math-academy-id "q-84976" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{2}{4})(x-1),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=1$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47616c55-81d5-53dd-a98b-37ede48b1258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555152} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4c4b5a-2d3b-5374-9935-fdd0f3bad0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{4{x}^{3}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555447} {:answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555532} {:answer/id #uuid "df40addd-09f3-5272-8d1e-ee10182960a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{3}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555545} {:answer/id #uuid "fce357ef-4288-5d72-8d01-538c32549ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{3}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555532} :answer-field/id #uuid "42914f58-aa47-51a0-8fbf-7284670805d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148557" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-3} & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y-3})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{3}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555096} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82c30e-c8c0-5715-961f-5c2888701119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{9}(x-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555178} {:answer/id #uuid "63a118d0-973e-503f-9dae-703dd3bf2be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64}(x-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555223} {:answer/id #uuid "8942b6cd-3961-531f-a8ba-bff58e7ddaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{8(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555319} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c318c9-9d14-5ca4-90e0-ce7b4d05d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8}{3(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555096} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b3eabf-5eed-56de-9552-c3437f08c08d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148745" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ x-5 & =-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<5,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555123} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9eedfb-df31-5fef-89f8-4a0a5bc39de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}z}{y}+6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555177} {:answer/id #uuid "738b92d9-e5fc-511d-904f-c37c0cfe4696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}z}{y}+3x{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555259} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e6de29-d31f-58b5-b882-4e6e62b0a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z}{y}+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555389} {:answer/id #uuid "e5810984-6602-54b8-9000-97b31b9afc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-4{x}^{4}z+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555123} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1ececc-599e-58e1-a6af-f6ea1d9aa196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-110680" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555189} :answer-field/id #uuid "75df9ea4-ee8b-50c0-8c6b-c1147f92edcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-270765" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{5}{2(x+3)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{5}{2(x+3)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ce7ecd-d0ed-5840-93f3-0001b823057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555045} {:answer/id #uuid "32707e5b-5823-5a1d-bd2b-ef75cdb2b376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555107} {:answer/id #uuid "760a156c-e362-5d1a-b5ea-f2896456664c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555267} {:answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555487} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4e4c09-aee9-5012-986e-68aab485dedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555487} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a9e958-7605-5553-8817-a7378b83215d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-33799" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf8bd7c7679e1c1df6c336b48892964c7658ac877b5efc22b099b1e98129d9ac.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343}{6}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343\\pi \\cdot 3}{6\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{343}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b92942c-8aba-5aa1-868e-af6ff0b747b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555050} {:answer/id #uuid "6332f8e1-70e3-52dc-ba10-2ad158c200d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555219} {:answer/id #uuid "6de35bfe-a94a-5f6f-a2a7-be882652597a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555251} {:answer/id #uuid "98125233-946f-554a-8efb-6031235b27b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555364} {:answer/id #uuid "f833dfc3-dd27-5cc6-a187-eb5f1348d40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b92942c-8aba-5aa1-868e-af6ff0b747b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555050} :answer-field/id #uuid "eada811c-d053-5120-a961-fe8ffd045312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b1a0e36-dce4-509e-90b0-4f68fe84ad7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84968" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+5)^{2}=-3(y-6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-5$ and $k=6.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-5,6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555025} {:answer/id #uuid "62d815c5-e833-5b48-87e0-0830919fd657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555218} {:answer/id #uuid "a010143e-ec2c-5360-b6bf-45e949bd9bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555386} {:answer/id #uuid "d918cea8-7679-5409-902e-13d46623dee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555524} {:answer/id #uuid "e42b10c9-8668-50fd-9c16-8cf7778d16eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555025} :answer-field/id #uuid "02aa06e3-c429-50fd-98e7-14828dfd8707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-148573" :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5,\\,x>-k\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-2,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{2}{3(x-5)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\nk=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9e3163-77a3-5b77-b6dd-5f9cc4183e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555102} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaceaf8-31cb-57d9-89f4-40f6fbb0f71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555164} {:answer/id #uuid "8720512d-ed7f-54db-9220-6ac8c52ec59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555313} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eb0642-fe00-5e0f-a9ab-9dcbf769b12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555520} {:answer/id #uuid "f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f334df-9edf-56f6-b904-ca6fc39afbf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-30452" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}-2x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=-2$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{-2}{2(4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0824024b-c099-527a-8256-d5a6127f2786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555013} {:answer/id #uuid "2f22d275-c75b-5b64-9666-30863a46ed54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555104} {:answer/id #uuid "4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555140} {:answer/id #uuid "76e3d3fa-3854-5450-9938-38191ec8e7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555274} {:answer/id #uuid "f412a256-5361-5b16-a302-db1d43b422fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555140} :answer-field/id #uuid "15067fae-69bc-5d5f-83e8-e1ebdc618e5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106134" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cd1c9ab91a23fae12dd45078ec44ca55e69da8aa787775d7a9583857a84cb71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{11}{6}$ and $\\frac{25}{6},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola." :question/worked-solution "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{25}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{25}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{36}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fdd0c6-1d98-54af-82c9-a3de113fe661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555016} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee61fd2-c684-57e7-9ea4-f09a8e349764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555027} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9c7297-d71f-5a15-82a4-809b7fbacfeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555379} {:answer/id #uuid "aa206b43-8b04-5867-a4d8-d7210015326b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555406} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d4718-a5a3-5cee-ad89-6d539fb4ecc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d4718-a5a3-5cee-ad89-6d539fb4ecc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555488} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b92ccf-c96a-52fb-b618-ecc3a61419d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d36205bb-47f5-5c37-9085-d080474849b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-131169" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30x-30}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{30}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{4{x}^{4}}+\\frac{30}{5{x}^{5}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{5}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30x-30}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{30}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{3{x}^{3}}+\\frac{30}{4{x}^{4}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{15}{2{x}^{4}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049d033e-9ea7-58bc-bb55-6b11185d1b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049d033e-9ea7-58bc-bb55-6b11185d1b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556352} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eaa9ff4-4551-5f33-ba72-289eaa72b4fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0921e1f0-6016-5a9a-b00a-281e162349d5" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa571435e7a44532cfb3bc61965b56b10e83c090236349851971e1b51c007ade.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=2\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{11}{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ b & =11\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=11\\,\\text{cm}.$"}]
